%[text] %[text:anchor:H_04B433ED] # Transverse-shear locking due incompatible discretization spaces
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] The transverse-shear locking can be attributed to the incompatibility of the discretization space for the vertical displacement and rotation fields. This manifests as residual transverse-shear forces for thin plates which trigger this numerical artifact. More information on this topic you can find [here](file:../4_TransverseShearLocking_TimoshenkoBeam/main_Chapter4_TransverseShearLocking_TimoshenkoBeam.mlx:H_8951AC12) based on the Timoshenko beam problem.
%[text] In this section the shear forces $q\_x = \\alpha G t \\gamma\_x = \\alpha G t \\overbrace{\\left( \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial X} + \\beta\_y \\right)}^{\\gamma\_x}$are computed using both the bilinear and the biquadratic elements along the line $Y = 2$ m.
%[text] 
%[text] Go to [THIS](file:.\main_Chapter4_Locking_ReissnerMindlinSquarePlate.m) Section in the main driver script *Transverse-Shear Locking in Shear-Deformable Plates* *`-`* *Two Sided Clampled Plate Subject to Vertical Distributed Load*
%[text] Go to [NEXT](file:.\main_Chapter42_ReissnerMindlinSquarePlate_SlendernessStudy.m) Section *Slenderness study on transverse shear-locking*
%[text] 
%[text] **Author: Andreas Apostolatos, PhD (aapostol@mathworks.com)**
%[text] **Date: 20.02.2023**
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F203EBCC] ## Preamble
vars = ["numPts" "mshQ1" "mshQ2" "propNewtonRaphson" "N_Q1" "N_Q2" ...
    "uQ1" "uQ2" "propStr"];
str = "This mlx-file should be run from parent mlx-file " + ...
    "main_Chapter01_OneDimensionalLinearBasisFunctions.mlx";
for ii = 1:length(vars)
    if ~exist(vars(ii), 'var') 
        error(strcat("Variable ", vars(ii), " is not defined. ", str));
    end
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B2326B68] ## Initialization of the evaluation points along the x-direction where the shear forces are computed
%[text] Define the vertical deflection $w(\\xi,\\eta)$, rotation $\\beta\_x,\\beta\_y$, position $\\mathbf{X}(\\xi,\\eta)$, jacobian $\\mathbf{J} (\\xi, \\eta)$, the derivative of the vertical deflection with respect to the physical space $\\frac{\\partial w (\\xi,\\eta)}{\\partial X}$, $\\frac{\\partial w (\\xi,\\eta)}{\\partial Y}$ and shear force $q\_x(\\xi, \\eta), q\_y(\\xi, \\eta)$ function fields
syms wQ1(xi, eta) wQ2(xi, eta) betaxQ1(xi, eta) betaxQ2(xi, eta) ...
    betayQ1(xi, eta) betayQ2(xi, eta) posVctQ1(xi, eta) ...
    posVctQ2(xi, eta) jacobianMtxQ1(xi, eta) jacobianMtxQ2(xi, eta) ...
    dwdxQ1(xi,eta) dwdxQ2(xi,eta) qxQ1(xi, eta) qxQ2(xi, eta)
%[text] Compute the bounding box of the Finite Element mesh and generate a sequence of sampling points onto which to compute the shear forces
boundingBox = [min(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1)) max(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1)) ...
    min(mshQ1.nodes(:, 2)) max(mshQ1.nodes(:, 2))];
yPts = repmat(mean(boundingBox(3:4)), numPts, 1);
xPts = linspace(boundingBox(1), boundingBox(2), numPts);
%[text] Array containing the shear forces at the evaluation points. The first column corresponds to the bilinear quadrilateral and the second column to the biquadratic quadrilateral mesh
qxPts = zeros(numPts, 2);
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B4190828] ## Loop over all the sampling points where the shear forces are computed
for ii = 1:numPts
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F003005C] ### Coordinates of the evaluation point
    coordPt = [xPts(ii) yPts(ii)];
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C14099F6] ### Element containing  the evaluation point
%[text] ![](text:image:8175) **Hint**
%[text] In case the point is found to be in more than one elements, then the first element found can be used for the computation of the shear force
    el = nan;
    for jj = 1:numEl
        in = inpolygon(coordPt(1),coordPt(2), ...
            mshQ1.nodes(mshQ1.elements(jj, :),1), ...
            mshQ1.nodes(mshQ1.elements(jj, :),2));
        if in
            el = jj;
            break;
        end
    end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A1EDB8BA] ### Verification of validity regarding the element containing the current point
%[text] ![](text:image:693d) **Hint**
%[text] The requested point might not belong to any of the elements in the mesh. In this case revise the coordinates of the evaluation point
    if isnan(el)
        error("The point with coordinates (%d, %d) was found in no element in the mesh", coordPt(1), coordPt(2));
    end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D7C34614] ### Local coordinates of the node in the element's parametric space
%[text] ![](text:image:3449) **Hint**
%[text] The Newton-Rapshon method is used in order to obtain the parametric coordinates of the evaluation point in the element where it belongs.
%[text] ![](text:image:3b09) **Reflect**
%[text] Why is it used a Newton-Rapshon method for obtaining the parametric coordinates of the evaluation point in the element where it belongs?
%[text] ![](text:image:1a70) **Reflect**
%[text] Are there alterative methods that can be used to solve this inverse problem? Name of one of them along with its advantages and disadvantages as compared to the Newton-Rapshon method
    [xiEta, ~] = computePointCoordinatesOnCanonicalBilinearQuadrilateral ...
        (coordPt', mshQ1.nodes(mshQ1.elements(el, :), 1:2)', ...
        propNewtonRaphson);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8CB0785F] ### Verification of the coordinates' validity in the element's parametric space
%[text] ![](text:image:98fe) **Hint**
%[text] The element's parametric space is defined in this case as $(\\xi, \\eta) \\in \[-1, 1\]^2$
    if xiEta(1, 1) < -1 - eps*1e2 || xiEta(1, 1) > 1 + eps*1e2 || ...
            xiEta(2, 1) < -1 - eps*1e2 || xiEta(2, 1) > 1 + eps*1e2
        error("The point with coordinates (%d, %d) is projected " + ...
            "onto element with id %d into local coordinates (%d, %d)", ...
            coordPt(1), coordPt(2), el, xiEta(1, 1), xiEta(2, 1));
    end
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7A117A6B] ### Element Freedom Table (EFT) for this element using the bilinear quadrilateral mesh
    EFTQ1 = [3*mshQ1.elements(el, :) - 2
             3*mshQ1.elements(el, :) - 1
             3*mshQ1.elements(el, :)];
    EFTQ1 = EFTQ1(:);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6ABC3399] ### Element Freedom Table (EFT) for this element using the biquadratic quadrilateral mesh
    EFTQ2 = [3*mshQ2.elements(el, :) - 2
             3*mshQ2.elements(el, :) - 1
             3*mshQ2.elements(el, :)];
    EFTQ2 = EFTQ2(:);
%[text] ![](text:image:1fb2) **Hint**
%[text] How should the DOFs be numbered in the Element Freedom Table (EFT)?
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_95692684] ### Vertical deflection and rotation fields for the bilinear quadrilateral element as symbolic functions
    wQ1(xi, eta) = N_Q1*uQ1(EFTQ1(1:3:end), 1); % w(x, y)
    betaxQ1(xi, eta) = N_Q1*uQ1(EFTQ1(2:3:end), 1); % beta_x(x, y)
    betayQ1(xi, eta) = N_Q1*uQ1(EFTQ1(3:3:end), 1); % beta_y(x, y)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_096A547D] ### Vertical deflection and rotation fields for the biquadratic quadrilateral element as symbolic functions
    wQ2(xi, eta) = N_Q2*uQ2(EFTQ2(1:3:end), 1); % w(x, y)
    betaxQ2(xi, eta) = N_Q2*uQ2(EFTQ2(2:3:end), 1); % beta_x(x, y)
    betayQ2(xi, eta) = N_Q2*uQ2(EFTQ2(3:3:end), 1); % beta_y(x, y)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_EAB62F18] ### Jacobian matrix at the element level as symbolic function
%[text] $\\left\[ \\begin{array}{c} X (\\xi, \\eta) \\\\ Y (\\xi, \\eta) \\end{array} \\right\] = \\mathbf{X}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\sum\_{i=1}^{n}N\_i (\\xi, \\eta) \\mathbf{X}\_i$
%[text] $\\mathbf{J}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\left\[ \\begin{array}{cc} \\frac{\\partial X}{\\partial \\xi} & \\frac{\\partial X}{\\partial \\eta} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial Y}{\\partial \\xi} & \\frac{\\partial Y}{\\partial \\eta} \\end{array} \\right\]$
%[text] ... for the the bilinear quadrilateral element
    posVctQ1(xi, eta) = N_Q1*mshQ1.nodes(mshQ1.elements(el, :), 1:2);
    jacobianMtxQ1(xi, eta) = jacobian(posVctQ1, [xi eta]);
%[text] ... for the biquadratic quadrilateral element
    posVctQ2(xi, eta) = N_Q2*mshQ2.nodes(mshQ2.elements(el, :), 1:2);
    jacobianMtxQ2(xi, eta) = jacobian(posVctQ2, [xi eta]);
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E09FA84F] ### Derivatives of the vertical deflection with respect to the physical space
%[text] ![](text:image:78d3) **Hint**
%[text] The vertical deflection and the cross-sectional rotation fields of the plate are defined in the parametric space of each element, since they are merely constructed as a linear combination of the basis functions times the Degrees of Freedom (DOFs), namely,
%[text] $w(\\xi,\\eta) = \\sum\_{i = 1}^n N\_i(\\xi, \\eta) w\_i \\\\\n\\beta\_x(\\xi,\\eta) = \\sum\_{i = 1}^n N\_i(\\xi, \\eta) \\beta\_{x,i} \\\\\n\\beta\_y(\\xi,\\eta) = \\sum\_{i = 1}^n N\_i(\\xi, \\eta) \\beta\_{y,i}$
%[text] It is sought to compute the derivatives of the latter quantities with respect to the physical space, namely, $\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial X}$, $\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial Y}$, because the computation of the shear forces requires that, namely,
%[text] $q\_x (\\xi, \\eta) = \\alpha G t \\gamma\_x = \\alpha G t \\overbrace{ \\left( \\frac{\\partial w }{\\partial X} + \\beta\_y \\right)}^{\\gamma\_x} \\\\\nq\_y (\\xi, \\eta) = \\alpha G t \\gamma\_y = \\alpha G t \\overbrace{ \\left( \\frac{\\partial w }{\\partial Y} - \\beta\_x \\right)}^{\\gamma\_y}$
%[text] To do so, the chain rule can be used, namely, $\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\xi} = \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial X} \\frac{\\partial X}{\\partial \\xi} + \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial Y} \\frac{\\partial X}{\\partial \\eta}$. By doing so and then inverting the system for both Cartesian derivatives one obtains the following relation,
%[text] $&dollar&;\\left\[ \\begin{array}{c} \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial X} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial Y} \\end{array} \\right\] = \\overbrace{\\left\[ \\begin{array}{cc} \\frac{\\partial \\xi}{\\partial X} & \\frac{\\partial \\eta}{\\partial X} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial \\xi}{\\partial Y} & \\frac{\\partial \\eta}{\\partial Y} \\end{array} \\right\]}^{\\left( \\mathbf{J}^{-1} \\right)^{\\text{T}} = \\mathbf{J}^{-\\text{T}}} \\left\[ \\begin{array}{c} \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\xi} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\eta} \\end{array} \\right\]&dollar&;$
%[text] where the the inverse of the Jacobian is defined then as follows,
%[text] $\\mathbf{J}^{-1}(\\xi, \\eta) = \\left\[ \\begin{array}{cc} \\frac{\\partial \\xi}{\\partial X} & \\frac{\\partial \\xi}{\\partial Y} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial \\eta}{\\partial X} & \\frac{\\partial \\eta}{\\partial Y} \\end{array} \\right\]$
%[text] Since we are interested in shear force $\\gamma\_x$, we seek to compute the derivative $\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial X}$
%[text] ... corresponding to the bilinear quadrilateral element
    dwdxQ1(xi, eta) = transpose(inv(jacobianMtxQ1(xi, eta)))* ...
        [diff(wQ1, xi); diff(wQ1, eta)];
%[text] ... corresponding to the biquadratic quadrilateral element
    dwdxQ2(xi, eta) = transpose(inv(jacobianMtxQ2(xi, eta)))* ...
        [diff(wQ2, xi); diff(wQ2, eta)];
%[text] ![](text:image:0e04) **Hint**
%[text] See how the [Symbolic Math Toolbox](https://www.mathworks.com/products/symbolic.html) can be herein nicely leverage to obtain analytical expressions without the need to perform differentiations by hand
%[text] ![](text:image:7f8d) **Try**
%[text] Try to change this workflow avoiding overall the use of the [Symbolic Math Toolbox](https://www.mathworks.com/products/symbolic.html). Which workflow renders the presentation of numerical methods in engineering more comprehensive?
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BFA5057A] ### Shear force along the *X*-direction
%[text] The formula for the computation of the shear forces along the X-direction is $q\_x (\\xi, \\eta) = \\alpha G t \\gamma\_x (\\xi,\\eta) = \\alpha G t \\overbrace{ \\frac{\\partial w (\\xi,\\eta)}{\\partial X} + \\beta\_y(\\xi, \\eta)}^{\\gamma\_x(\\xi, \\eta)}$. The evaluation of this formula for the different element types is provided below
%[text] ... for the bilinear quadrilateral elements
    qxQ1(xi, eta) = propStr.alpha*propStr.G*propStr.t* ...
        ([1 0]*dwdxQ1(xi, eta) + betayQ1(xi, eta));
    qxPts(ii, 1) = double(qxQ1(xiEta(1, 1), xiEta(2, 1)));
%[text] ... for the bilinear quadrilateral elements
    qxQ2(xi, eta) = propStr.alpha*propStr.G*propStr.t* ...
        ([1 0]*dwdxQ2(xi, eta) + betayQ2(xi, eta));
    qxPts(ii, 2) = double(qxQ2(xiEta(1, 1), xiEta(2, 1)));
%[text] 
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C87A9890] ## Shear force distribution along the *X*-direction at the middle of the plate
numVertices = numel(mshQ1.nodes(:, 1));
plotMesh2d(mshQ1, numVertices, false); %[output:795720ad]
hold on; %[output:795720ad]
plot3(xPts, yPts, qxPts(:, 1), 'b-', 'LineWidth',2); %[output:795720ad]
plot3(xPts, yPts, qxPts(:, 2), 'r-', 'LineWidth',2); %[output:795720ad]
scalVer = (max(abs(qxPts(:))) - min(abs(qxPts(:))))/2; % scaling for the support dimensions along the Z-direction
[xsQ1, ysQ1, zsQ1] = createSupports3D(mshCurrQ1.nodes, homDOFsQ1, scalVer);
for k = 1:length(xsQ1(:, 1))
    plot3(xsQ1(k, :), ysQ1(k, :), zsQ1(k, :), 'Linewidth', 2, 'Color', 'black'); %[output:795720ad]
end
qxKirchhoffPlate = load("./4_TransverseShearLocking/" + ...
    "4_TransverseShearLocking_ReissnerMindlinSquarePlate/" + ...
    "qx_KirchhoffPlate.mat", "P");
qx = qxKirchhoffPlate.P;
qx(:, 2) = qx(:, 2) + YLy/2;
plot3(qx(:, 1), qx(:, 2), qx(:, 3), LineWidth=2, Color='k'); %[output:795720ad]
hold off; %[output:795720ad]
xlabel("$X$", "Interpreter", "latex") %[output:795720ad]
ylabel("$Y$", "Interpreter", "latex") %[output:795720ad]
zlabel("$q_x$", "Interpreter", "latex") %[output:795720ad]
legend("", "", "Q1", "Q2", "Kirchhoff theory", Location="best") %[output:795720ad]
title("Shear forces $q_x$ along the $X$-direction at $Y=2$ of " + ... %[output:group:6ee5fbe7] %[output:795720ad]
    "the plate", "Interpreter", "latex") %[output:group:6ee5fbe7] %[output:795720ad]
%[text] ![](text:image:900b) **Reflect**
%[text] How was the reference solution obtained for this problem? What would you expect for moderately thick and thick plates in this case?

